            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week’s Observation: Many Interruptions Do Not Occur on Launch Day</h2>
<p>The key issue this week is not a new platform feature, but the continued failure of many teams to treat balance alerts as part of campaign risk control. A common approach is to set a fixed threshold, such as notifying finance when the remaining balance falls below $10,000. However, an advertiser may operate multiple accounts, currencies, and billing cycles, so the displayed balance does not always represent fully available funds.</p>
<p>A more reliable metric is <strong>estimated days of runway</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Days of runway = Available account balance ÷ Average daily spend over the last seven days</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the balance is expected to last less than half a day, action should be taken immediately even if the account still appears adequately funded. Conversely, a high balance may not solve the problem if credit is about to expire or the billing method is restricted. Recharging alone is not a complete risk-control strategy.</p>
<h2>Use Three Alert Levels Instead of One Fixed Threshold</h2>
<p><strong>Level 1: Planning notice.</strong> Notify the campaign owner when the balance is expected to last 7–10 days. The owner should review schedules, creatives, and planned budget changes.</p>
<p><strong>Level 2: Finance notice.</strong> Trigger a payment request when the estimated runway falls to 3–5 days. Include the account ID, currency, projected shortfall, and payment deadline.</p>
<p><strong>Level 3: Interruption risk alert.</strong> Escalate immediately when the balance will last fewer than two days, or when a payment or credit status issue appears.</p>
<p>These thresholds should not be copied without adjustment. Accounts entering a scaling phase usually burn budget faster and need a longer buffer. Stable accounts with lower daily spend can use shorter notice periods. For advertisers managing multiple accounts, the team should also distinguish between an adequate total balance and insufficient funds in a specific account. A consolidated dashboard can otherwise hide local risks.</p>
<h2>Add Funding Timelines to the Campaign Schedule</h2>
<p>Finance teams often begin processing a recharge only after an urgent alert arrives. However, the period from request to payment and platform confirmation requires additional buffer time. In one anonymous tool app campaign, the team reviewed its planned scaling date, spend over the previous three days, and payment processing cycle before launch. This prevented the primary account from being interrupted by a delayed debit.</p>
<p>Every recharge request should record four details: request time, expected settlement time, target account, and usable date. If funds are expected to arrive before a campaign starts, they should not be verified only on launch day. When splitting a recharge across several accounts, prioritize the accounts with the highest scheduled budgets and allocate funds to clearly defined campaigns or ad sets.</p>
<p>Teams should also avoid excessive payment-method changes, repeated retries after a failed transaction, or sudden changes to the funding route, as these actions may trigger additional review. When a debit fails, check the failure reason, payment method, and account restrictions before retrying or switching payment channels.</p>
<h2>Run a Weekly “Spend Forecast Meets Settlement” Check</h2>
<p>During weekly reconciliation, review a concise funding checklist:</p>
<ul>
<li>Current available balance and any funds under review;</li>
<li>Average daily spend over the last seven and 30 days;</li>
<li>Budget scheduled for the next two weeks;</li>
<li>Payments pending or submitted but not yet credited;</li>
<li>Credit status, billing method, and payment restrictions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Compare this information with the next two weeks of campaign plans. That makes it possible to identify timing gaps where money is still in transit while campaigns are expected to launch.</p>
<p>The value of balance alerts is not another notification. It is to bring finance, campaign management, and account operations onto the same risk sheet. The real goal is not simply to avoid running out of funds, but to prevent a team from discovering, after budgets and creatives are finalized, that the money has not arrived in time.</p>
